<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00NR">Definition <span data-tag="00NR">2.5.1.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{A}}$ be an additive category, let $C_{\ast }$ be a chain complex with values in $\operatorname{\mathcal{A}}$, and let $n$ be an integer. We will say that $C_{\ast }$ is <em>concentrated in degrees $\geq n$</em> if objects $C_{m} \in \operatorname{\mathcal{A}}$ are zero for $m &lt; n$. Similarly, we say that $C_{\ast }$ is <em>concentrated in degrees $\leq n$</em> if the objects $C_ m$ are zero for $m &gt; n$. We let $\operatorname{Ch}(\operatorname{\mathcal{A}})_{\geq n}$ denote the full subcategory of $\operatorname{Ch}(\operatorname{\mathcal{A}})$ spanned by those chain complexes which are concentrated in degrees $\geq n$, and $\operatorname{Ch}(\operatorname{\mathcal{A}})_{\leq n}$ the full subcategory spanned by those chain complexes which are concentrated in degrees $\leq n$. </p>
